add("deep-questions-to-ask-your-boyfriend","q","52 Deep Questions to Ask Your Boyfriend (That Don't Feel Like an Interview)",
"Thoughtful questions to ask your boyfriend or husband: childhood, dreams, money, love and silly ones for road trips.","Talk & Connection","7","mid",
"<h3>When the questions open something up</h3><p>Some answers surprise you. If you want to understand what is going on underneath his answers, one guide explains it better than most.</p>",
"""<p>The best conversation my husband and I had last year happened in a parked car outside a Safeway, because I asked him one odd question: <em>what did you want to be at nine years old?</em> Forty minutes later the ice cream was soft and I knew things about him I had not heard in twelve years.</p>
<p>Good questions do that. They are not a quiz. Pick two or three, ask them when you are side by side (driving, walking, cooking), and let silence do some of the work.</p>
<h2>Childhood and roots</h2><ol><li>What did you want to be at nine?</li><li>Which adult made you feel most understood as a kid?</li><li>What is a smell that sends you straight back to childhood?</li><li>What rule in your house growing up seems strange now?</li><li>What were you proud of that nobody noticed?</li><li>Who taught you how to be a man, for better or worse?</li><li>What holiday tradition would you keep forever?</li></ol>
<h2>Dreams and the future</h2><ol start="8"><li>If money were handled, how would you spend a Tuesday?</li><li>What is a skill you secretly want to learn?</li><li>Where do you picture us at seventy?</li><li>What would you regret not trying?</li><li>Which of your goals do you talk about least?</li><li>What does a "good year" look like to you?</li><li>What would make you feel successful, in your own words?</li></ol>
<h2>Love and us</h2><ol start="15"><li>When did you first feel at home with me?</li><li>What small thing I do makes you feel respected?</li><li>What do you wish I asked you about more often?</li><li>How do you know when I'm upset, before I say it?</li><li>What is one way I could support you this month?</li><li>Which memory of us do you replay most?</li><li>What does feeling needed mean to you?</li></ol>
<p>That last one matters more than it sounds. A lot of men will never volunteer it, but feeling useful and trusted is close to the center of how they attach. I wrote more about that idea in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">honest review of His Secret Obsession</a>, which is built around it.</p>
<h2>Money and work</h2><ol start="22"><li>What did your parents teach you about money without meaning to?</li><li>Saver or spender, honestly?</li><li>What job taught you the most?</li><li>What would you do if you could quit tomorrow?</li><li>What purchase still makes you happy?</li><li>What is a money fear you have never said out loud?</li></ol>
<h2>Light and silly (road-trip safe)</h2><ol start="28"><li>Which fictional character are you most like?</li><li>What is the worst haircut you ever had?</li><li>What would your wrestling entrance song be?</li><li>Pancakes or waffles, and defend it.</li><li>Which animal would you be for a day?</li><li>What is a hill you would die on?</li><li>What food could you eat every day?</li><li>What would your perfect sandwich be?</li><li>Best concert you ever went to?</li><li>Which decade would you live in?</li></ol>
<h2>For slow evenings</h2><ol start="38"><li>What are you most grateful for this week?</li><li>When do you feel most like yourself?</li><li>What is something you have changed your mind about?</li><li>Who do you miss?</li><li>What do you want more of in your life?</li><li>What advice would you give your twenty-year-old self?</li><li>What scares you a little about getting older?</li><li>What makes a friendship last?</li><li>What is your favorite way to be comforted?</li><li>When was the last time you cried?</li><li>What song feels like us?</li><li>What would you like to be remembered for?</li><li>What is a small win you never celebrated?</li><li>What do you love about your own life right now?</li><li>What question do you wish I had asked?</li></ol>
<h2>How to ask without it feeling heavy</h2><p>One question per evening is plenty. Answer it yourself too. If he gives a short answer, try "say more about that" once, then let it go. Curiosity is the point, not completion.</p>""")

add("cozy-date-night-ideas-at-home","dn","21 Cozy Date Night Ideas at Home for Fall and Winter",
"Cheap, cozy at-home date night ideas for couples: themed dinners, blanket forts, game nights and no-screen evenings.","Date Nights","6","end",
"<h3>Turn date night into a habit he looks forward to</h3><p>The ideas are the easy part. Knowing what actually makes a man feel drawn in is the part I found in one program.</p>",
"""<p>Monsoon season ended here in Tucson, the evenings cooled off, and suddenly the couch is the best restaurant in town. These are the at-home dates we actually use, sorted by effort.</p>
<h2>Under 15 minutes of prep</h2><ul><li><b>Blanket and stars.</b> Drag the comforter onto the patio or a balcony, bring cocoa, find one constellation together.</li><li><b>Childhood movie swap.</b> Each of you picks the movie that shaped you as a kid. Explain why before pressing play.</li><li><b>Candle-only dinner.</b> Same takeout, zero overhead lights. It changes the whole mood.</li><li><b>Question jar.</b> Write ten questions each, fold, draw. (Our <a href="/deep-questions-to-ask-your-boyfriend/">list of deep questions</a> is a good starting pile.)</li><li><b>Old photos night.</b> Scroll back to your first year together and narrate.</li></ul>
<h2>A little effort, big payoff</h2><ul><li><b>Passport dinner.</b> Pick a country, cook one dish, play its music, learn three words.</li><li><b>Fondue or hot pot.</b> Slow food forces slow talking.</li><li><b>Fort night.</b> Yes, a blanket fort. Adults love it more than kids.</li><li><b>Paint-and-sip.</b> Cheap canvases, a YouTube tutorial, terrible art, great laughs.</li><li><b>Backyard s'mores bar.</b> Fancy chocolate, cookies instead of graham crackers, a small fire bowl.</li><li><b>Board game tournament.</b> Best of three, loser makes breakfast.</li></ul>
<h2>Seasonal ones</h2><ul><li><b>Pumpkin carving with a twist:</b> carve each other's faces.</li><li><b>Hot cider tasting:</b> three store-bought ciders, rank them blind.</li><li><b>Holiday cookie bake-off</b> with silly judging categories.</li><li><b>Light-looking drive</b> with a thermos and a playlist from the year you met.</li><li><b>Winter soup night:</b> one of you makes the soup, the other makes bread.</li></ul>
<h2>When you are both exhausted</h2><ul><li><b>Bath and audiobook.</b> One chapter, then bed.</li><li><b>Foot rub trade</b> with a ten-minute timer each.</li><li><b>Phones-in-a-drawer hour.</b> Just talk. It feels awkward for five minutes, then it doesn't.</li><li><b>Plan a dream trip</b> you may never take, down to the hotel.</li><li><b>Breakfast for dinner</b> in pajamas.</li></ul>
<h2>What makes a date night work</h2><p>It is not the activity. It is attention. A man who feels admired and wanted shows up differently, even on a pajama night. The small things you say during a date often matter more than the plan. That idea comes up again and again in what I read about male attraction, and it changed how I approach evenings in.</p>""")

add("five-love-languages-explained","ll","The 5 Love Languages Explained (With Real Examples for Couples)",
"A simple guide to the five love languages: words, time, gifts, acts of service and touch, with everyday examples.","Talk & Connection","6","mid",
"<h3>Love languages are a start, not the whole map</h3><p>They describe how people receive love. What they don't explain is the deeper drive behind how many men commit. This guide goes into that part.</p>",
"""<p>The love languages idea is simple: people feel loved in different ways, and we tend to give love the way <em>we</em> want to receive it. That mismatch explains a lot of "but I do so much for you" arguments.</p>
<h2>1. Words of affirmation</h2><p>Spoken or written appreciation. Examples: a note in the lunch bag, "I'm proud of how you handled that," telling a friend something good about him while he can hear.</p>
<h2>2. Quality time</h2><p>Undivided attention. A walk without phones, cooking together, asking a question and listening to the whole answer.</p>
<h2>3. Receiving gifts</h2><p>Not about price. It's about being thought of. A favorite candy bar from the gas station counts.</p>
<h2>4. Acts of service</h2><p>Doing things that lighten the load: warming up the car, handling the call he hates making, folding the laundry he started.</p>
<h2>5. Physical touch</h2><p>Hand on the back while passing in the kitchen, sitting close on the couch, long hugs.</p>
<h2>How to find yours (and his)</h2><ul><li>Notice what you complain about most. It often points to what you're missing.</li><li>Notice how he shows love. People usually give in their own language.</li><li>Ask: "What's something I did this week that made you feel cared for?"</li></ul>
<h2>Where it gets more interesting</h2><p>Many men don't fit neatly in one box. Under the language, there is often a need to feel respected, trusted and useful to the person they love. When that need is met, the love languages almost take care of themselves. I looked at one program built around that need in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">His Secret Obsession review</a>, and it filled in what this framework leaves out.</p>
<h2>A one-week experiment</h2><ol><li>Each day, do one small thing in his language.</li><li>Don't announce it.</li><li>At the end of the week, notice how he responds, and ask him to try it for you.</li></ol>""")

add("keep-the-spark-alive-long-term","sp","How to Keep the Spark Alive in a Long-Term Relationship",
"Practical ways to keep romance going after years together: novelty, appreciation, flirting and small daily rituals.","Long-Term Love","7","end",
"<h3>The spark has a reason behind it</h3><p>Novelty helps, but there's a deeper trigger that makes a man feel pulled toward you again. One relationship coach explains it in plain language.</p>",
"""<p>Twelve years in, I can tell you the spark doesn't vanish. It gets buried under groceries, school pickups and who forgot to pay the water bill. These habits are how we dig it back out.</p>
<h2>Do new things together</h2><p>Novelty releases the same chemistry you had at the start. It doesn't have to be skydiving. A new hiking trail, a cooking class, a different coffee shop on Saturday. New places make you see each other fresh.</p>
<h2>Flirt like nobody is keeping score</h2><p>Send the text you would have sent in year one. Wink across the kitchen. Compliment something specific: the way he fixed the gate, how he talks to your mom.</p>
<h2>Keep a six-second kiss</h2><p>A quick peck is a habit. A slow six-second kiss goodbye is a moment. Try it for a week.</p>
<h2>Protect a weekly date</h2><p>Same night, every week, even if it's at home. (<a href="/cozy-date-night-ideas-at-home/">Here are 21 ideas</a> for evenings in.)</p>
<h2>Admire him out loud</h2><p>Men rarely hear they are admired. When you tell him what you respect about him, you feed something that keeps him close. I explain this idea, and the program built around it, in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">review of His Secret Obsession</a>.</p>
<h2>Have a life of your own</h2><p>Your own friends, hobbies and goals make you more interesting to come home to. Mystery is not games, it's having a full life.</p>
<h2>Repair fast</h2><p>Every couple fights. Long-term couples just repair faster: "I'm sorry I snapped. Can we start over?"</p>
<h2>Small rituals that add up</h2><ul><li>Coffee together before phones.</li><li>A two-minute check-in at dinner: high and low of the day.</li><li>A shared playlist you both add to.</li><li>Holding hands on walks.</li></ul>""")

add("signs-he-is-pulling-away","pa","Signs He's Pulling Away (and What Actually Helps)",
"Common signs a boyfriend or husband is pulling away emotionally, why it happens, and calm steps that help.","Understanding Him","7","mid",
"<h3>Want to understand why he goes quiet?</h3><p>The guide I keep recommending explains the pull-back from a man's side, without games or manipulation.</p>",
"""<p>The texts get shorter. He's on his phone at dinner. He says "I'm fine" in that voice. It's scary, and the first instinct is to chase. Before you do, slow down.</p>
<h2>Common signs</h2><ul><li>Less curiosity about your day.</li><li>Fewer plans for the future, even small ones.</li><li>More time alone, at work or in the garage.</li><li>Less physical affection.</li><li>Conversations stay on logistics.</li></ul>
<h2>Why it often happens</h2><p>Stress at work, money worries, health, feeling criticized, or feeling like he's not needed. Many men withdraw to handle things alone before they talk. Pulling away is not always about you, and it's not always the end.</p>
<h2>What doesn't help</h2><ul><li>Long texts demanding answers.</li><li>Checking his phone.</li><li>Pretending you don't care.</li></ul>
<h2>What helps</h2><ol><li><b>Name it gently once.</b> "You seem far away lately. I'm here if you want to talk." Then stop.</li><li><b>Give space without disappearing.</b> Stay warm and normal.</li><li><b>Ask for his help with something real.</b> Feeling needed is a powerful pull for many men.</li><li><b>Fill your own cup.</b> Friends, exercise, sleep.</li><li><b>Notice effort and thank him.</b> Specific appreciation lands.</li></ol>
<p>The idea that men attach through feeling needed and admired is the core of a program I reviewed in detail. If his distance is confusing, <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">read what I thought of it</a>.</p>
<h2>When it's more than a phase</h2><p>If there's disrespect, lying, or you feel unsafe, that's not something to fix alone. Talk to a counselor, and if you're in danger, call the National Domestic Violence Hotline at 1-800-799-7233.</p>""")

add("communicate-without-fighting","cm","How to Talk to Your Partner Without It Turning Into a Fight",
"Simple communication tips for couples: soft starts, timing, I-statements and how to cool down mid-argument.","Talk & Connection","6","end",
"<h3>Words that make him lean in</h3><p>Some phrases make a man defensive, others make him want to fix things with you. One program lays out which is which.</p>",
"""<p>Most of our fights were never about the dishes. They were about how I brought up the dishes. These are the habits that cut our arguments in half.</p>
<h2>Start soft</h2><p>The first sentence predicts how the talk will go. "You never help" starts a war. "I'm overwhelmed tonight, can you take the dishes?" starts a fix.</p>
<h2>Pick your moment</h2><p>Not when he walks in the door. Not when either of you is hungry. Ask, "Is now a good time to talk about something small?"</p>
<h2>Use one complaint at a time</h2><p>Don't stack the last six months onto tonight.</p>
<h2>Say what you need, not just what's wrong</h2><p>"I need ten minutes of hearing about your day" is something he can do. "You don't pay attention to me" is something he can only defend against.</p>
<h2>Take a real break</h2><p>If your heart is pounding, take 20 minutes. Say when you'll come back. Then do.</p>
<h2>Repair attempts count</h2><p>A joke, a touch, "okay, I hear you" — accept them when he offers.</p>
<h2>Respect is the secret ingredient</h2><p>Many men hear criticism as "you're failing me." Pairing a request with respect ("you're so good at this, could you…") changes the reaction completely. It's the main idea behind a guide I wrote about in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">His Secret Obsession review</a>.</p>
<h2>Practice line</h2><p>"I feel ___ when ___. What I'd love is ___. Can we try that?"</p>""")

add("fall-date-ideas-for-couples","fd","30 Fall Date Ideas for Couples (Cheap, Cozy and Outdoorsy)",
"Fun fall date ideas: pumpkin patches, scenic drives, apple picking, haunted tours and cozy nights in.","Date Nights","5","mid",
"<h3>Make the season feel like the beginning again</h3><p>Fall dates are lovely. Pair them with what actually builds attraction and they stick. Here's the guide I used.</p>",
"""<p>Fall in the desert comes late, but when it hits, we go out every weekend. Here are thirty ideas, mixed between free, cheap and splurge.</p>
<h2>Outdoors</h2><ol><li>Pumpkin patch and corn maze.</li><li>Apple or pear picking.</li><li>Scenic drive with a thermos of cider.</li><li>Sunset hike and picnic.</li><li>Fall farmers market brunch.</li><li>Bonfire with friends.</li><li>Leaf-peeping road trip.</li><li>Outdoor movie night.</li><li>Hayride.</li><li>Stargazing on a clear cold night.</li></ol>
<h2>Out on the town</h2><ol start="11"><li>Haunted house or ghost tour.</li><li>Oktoberfest-style beer garden.</li><li>Wine or cider tasting.</li><li>Fall festival or craft fair.</li><li>Cozy bookstore and coffee.</li><li>Pottery class.</li><li>High school football game.</li><li>Local theater play.</li><li>Thrift-store challenge: $10 each for the other's outfit.</li><li>Try a new soup place every week.</li></ol>
<h2>Cozy at home</h2><ol start="21"><li>Carve pumpkins.</li><li>Bake a pie from scratch.</li><li>Horror movie marathon.</li><li>Make caramel apples.</li><li>Puzzle and cocoa.</li><li>Write each other a letter to open next fall.</li><li>Plan Thanksgiving together.</li><li>Rake leaves, then jump in them.</li><li>Candle-making kit.</li><li>Read the same book and talk about it.</li></ol>
<p>Want more indoor ideas? Here's my list of <a href="/cozy-date-night-ideas-at-home/">cozy date nights at home</a>. And if you want to know why some dates make him feel closer than others, I covered that in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">His Secret Obsession review</a>.</p>""")

add("thoughtful-christmas-gifts-for-him","gf","Thoughtful Christmas Gifts for Him That Aren't Socks",
"Christmas gift ideas for your boyfriend or husband: experiences, personal gifts, practical upgrades and gifts under $30.","Holidays Together","6","end",
"<h3>The gift that isn't in a box</h3><p>The thing many men want most is to feel admired and needed. One relationship guide shows how to give that every day.</p>",
"""<p>Every December I stare at my husband's wish list (it says "nothing") and panic. Here's what's worked better than socks.</p>
<h2>Experiences</h2><ul><li>Tickets to see his team or a band he loved in high school.</li><li>A class: knife skills, woodworking, BBQ.</li><li>A night away within driving distance.</li><li>A tasting: hot sauce, whiskey, coffee.</li></ul>
<h2>Personal and sentimental</h2><ul><li>A letter listing twelve things you admire about him.</li><li>A photo book of your year.</li><li>An engraved pocket knife or watch.</li><li>His grandma's recipe, framed.</li><li>A star map of the night you met.</li></ul>
<h2>Practical upgrades</h2><ul><li>Good slippers or a heavy robe.</li><li>A quality flashlight or multi-tool.</li><li>A better wallet.</li><li>Wireless earbuds for the gym.</li><li>A cast iron skillet.</li></ul>
<h2>Under $30</h2><ul><li>His favorite snacks in a custom box.</li><li>Coupon book with real, specific offers.</li><li>A mug with an inside joke.</li><li>A puzzle of a photo of you two.</li></ul>
<h2>The gift underneath</h2><p>That letter of things you admire? It's often the gift he keeps. Feeling appreciated and respected is a huge need for many men, and few people say it out loud. If that idea interests you, my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">review of His Secret Obsession</a> explains the concept in more detail.</p>""")

add("sweet-texts-to-send-him","tx","40 Sweet Texts to Send Him (Good Morning, Flirty and Just Because)",
"Cute texts to send your boyfriend or husband: good morning messages, flirty texts, support texts and appreciation notes.","Understanding Him","5","mid",
"<h3>Why some texts land harder</h3><p>Certain kinds of messages tap into how men bond. A relationship coach breaks it down if you want the why.</p>",
"""<p>A text takes six seconds and can change his whole afternoon. Steal these, change the words to sound like you.</p>
<h2>Good morning</h2><ol><li>Woke up thinking about you. Good start to a day.</li><li>Coffee tastes better when you make it. Just saying.</li><li>Go get 'em today. I believe in you.</li><li>Morning, handsome.</li><li>Already looking forward to tonight.</li><li>Hope today is kind to you.</li><li>You looked so peaceful sleeping. Didn't want to wake you.</li><li>Big day? You've got this.</li></ol>
<h2>Flirty</h2><ol start="9"><li>Can't stop thinking about last night.</li><li>Wear the blue shirt tonight.</li><li>I have a surprise for you later.</li><li>You're very distracting, even from across town.</li><li>Guess what I'm thinking about.</li><li>Come home early?</li><li>You still give me butterflies.</li><li>Dinner's on me. Dessert too.</li></ol>
<h2>Support</h2><ol start="17"><li>Proud of you for handling that.</li><li>Whatever happens today, I'm on your side.</li><li>You don't have to fix it tonight. Rest.</li><li>Thank you for working so hard for us.</li><li>I trust your judgment on this.</li><li>Need anything? I'm here.</li><li>You're stronger than this week.</li><li>Call if you want to vent. No advice, just ears.</li></ol>
<p>Notice how many of those say "I trust you" or "I'm proud of you." Those are the texts men tend to remember. I go into why in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">honest His Secret Obsession review</a>.</p>
<h2>Just because</h2><ol start="25"><li>Remember our first date? I do.</li><li>Saw a dog that looked like you. Cute.</li><li>Our song came on in the store.</li><li>Thanks for fixing the sink. My hero.</li><li>I like you. A lot.</li><li>You make ordinary days good.</li><li>Grocery list: milk, eggs, you.</li><li>Lucky me.</li></ol>
<h2>Appreciation</h2><ol start="33"><li>Thank you for always making me laugh.</li><li>You're a great dad.</li><li>I noticed you took care of that. Thank you.</li><li>I'd choose you again.</li><li>You make me feel safe.</li><li>I love how you love our family.</li><li>You're my favorite person to do nothing with.</li><li>Home is wherever you are.</li></ol>""")

add("make-him-feel-appreciated","ap","15 Ways to Make Your Husband Feel Appreciated",
"Small, specific ways to show appreciation to your husband or boyfriend, from words to actions he'll actually notice.","Long-Term Love","5","end",
"<h3>Appreciation, the deep version</h3><p>There's a reason specific appreciation hits so hard for men. One guide explains that instinct and how to speak to it.</p>",
"""<p>Most husbands I know aren't asking for grand gestures. They want to be noticed for what they already do. Here are fifteen ways, none expensive.</p>
<ol><li><b>Thank him for invisible work.</b> Taking out the trash, checking the tire pressure, handling the car insurance.</li><li><b>Brag about him to others</b>, where he can hear.</li><li><b>Ask his advice</b> on something you actually value his opinion about.</li><li><b>Let him finish the story</b> without correcting the details.</li><li><b>Leave a note</b> in his truck or laptop bag.</li><li><b>Make his favorite meal</b> on a random Wednesday.</li><li><b>Protect his downtime.</b> Give him an hour alone with no guilt.</li><li><b>Say "you were right."</b> It costs nothing.</li><li><b>Notice effort, not just results.</b></li><li><b>Hug him first</b> when he gets home.</li><li><b>Text him a specific compliment</b> (<a href="/sweet-texts-to-send-him/">40 ready-made texts here</a>).</li><li><b>Back him up in public</b>, disagree in private.</li><li><b>Show interest in his hobby</b>, even the boring one.</li><li><b>Plan a date around what he loves.</b></li><li><b>Tell him why you chose him.</b></li></ol>
<h2>Why this works</h2><p>Feeling appreciated is tied closely to feeling needed, and for many men that's the thread that holds attachment together. My <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">His Secret Obsession review</a> covers this idea and whether the program is worth it.</p>""")

add("couples-journal-prompts","jp","Relationship Journal Prompts for Couples (Printable-Friendly)",
"Journal prompts for couples to reflect, reconnect and plan: gratitude, memories, conflict and future dreams.","Long-Term Love","6","mid",
"<h3>Writing helps you see him differently</h3><p>If the prompts stir up questions about what he really wants, this coach's guide gives a clear answer.</p>",
"""<p>We started a shared notebook on the kitchen counter two years ago. Some weeks we both write, some weeks nobody does. It still became one of my favorite parts of us.</p>
<h2>How to use these</h2><p>One prompt each per week. Write separately, then read each other's answers on Sunday morning. No arguing about answers. Just "thank you for writing that."</p>
<h2>Gratitude</h2><ul><li>Three things you did this week that I noticed.</li><li>A moment I felt lucky to be with you.</li><li>Something you do that makes my life easier.</li></ul>
<h2>Memories</h2><ul><li>The first time I knew I loved you.</li><li>Our funniest disaster.</li><li>A trip I'd repeat exactly the same.</li></ul>
<h2>Understanding each other</h2><ul><li>When I feel most loved by you.</li><li>Something I wish you understood about my day.</li><li>When I feel most respected by you.</li><li>What I need when I'm stressed.</li></ul>
<p>That "respected" prompt is the one my husband took longest to answer, and his answer surprised me. It's closely related to the idea I wrote about in my <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">review of His Secret Obsession</a>.</p>
<h2>Conflict and repair</h2><ul><li>An argument I think we handled well.</li><li>One thing I want to do better next time.</li><li>A phrase that calms me down.</li></ul>
<h2>Future</h2><ul><li>One thing I want us to try this year.</li><li>Our home in ten years.</li><li>A tradition I want to start.</li></ul>
<p>Need conversation starters too? Try our <a href="/deep-questions-to-ask-your-boyfriend/">52 deep questions</a>.</p>""")

add("healthy-boundaries-in-relationships","hb","Healthy Boundaries in Relationships: Examples and How to Set Them",
"What healthy boundaries look like in a relationship, examples for time, money, phones and family, and how to say them kindly.","Understanding Him","6","end",
"<h3>Boundaries and attraction go together</h3><p>A woman with a full life and clear boundaries is magnetic. One guide explains how that fits with what makes men commit.</p>",
"""<p>Boundaries used to sound cold to me. Now I think of them as instructions for loving me well, and asking him for his.</p>
<h2>What a boundary is (and isn't)</h2><p>A boundary is about what <em>you</em> will do. "If the yelling starts, I'll leave the room and we can talk later." It isn't a rule for him: "You can't see your friends."</p>
<h2>Examples by area</h2><ul><li><b>Time:</b> "I need Sunday mornings for myself."</li><li><b>Phones:</b> "No phones at dinner, for both of us."</li><li><b>Money:</b> "Let's talk before any purchase over $200."</li><li><b>Family:</b> "We decide holiday plans together before telling our parents."</li><li><b>Emotional:</b> "I'm not okay being teased about my weight, even as a joke."</li><li><b>Physical:</b> "I need a few minutes of quiet after work before hugs."</li></ul>
<h2>How to say it kindly</h2><ol><li>Pick a calm moment (see <a href="/communicate-without-fighting/">how to talk without fighting</a>).</li><li>Share the why.</li><li>Keep it short.</li><li>Ask about his.</li><li>Follow through gently.</li></ol>
<h2>Red flags</h2><p>If your boundaries are mocked, punished or constantly pushed, that's a pattern worth taking to a counselor.</p>
<h2>Why boundaries make you more attractive</h2><p>Self-respect is attractive. A man who sees you value yourself tends to value you more. That's one of the ideas I found in a relationship program I reviewed. <a href="/his-secret-obsession-review/">Here's my full take</a>.</p>""")
